		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Is it possible to increase modulation rate?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4499&amp;p=34674#p34674"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hi all,<br><br>I have 2 DM1701, both are power and frequency calibrated.<br>1 DM1701 works as a hotspot: Pi-star on a pi-nano-neo<br>RF power on both sides is 250mW (same results with 50 mW and 500mW)<br>BER is less than 1% but in parrot mode the echoed voice is quite unintelligible, I suspect that the modulation rate is too low because the signal strength displayed in the pistar console is never more than S4.<br><br>Is anyone has an idea to increase the signal strength ?<br><br>Thanks and 73!<br>Hubert / F8LJV</div></blockquote>No. There is no way in the firmware to adjust this in the firmware, but unless your radio's factory calibration data is incorrect there should be no need to do this.<br><br>It is not likely that the factory calibration is incorrect. <br><br>Its more likely the problem is the frequency calibration of each radio is not the same.   <br><br>Did you check the frequency calibration. It needs to be accurate to about +/- 100Hz<br><br>You don't need a frequency counter to do this. You only need to guarantee both radios transmit on exactly the same frequency; you can do this using a SDR to view the exact frequeny of the radio which has the lowest BER, then adjust the other other radio to that frequency.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Sun Dec 15, 2024 6:51 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
